[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I reproduced connectivity between the two PCs and found no fault in the network. From PC15 to PC16 and back, ICMP succeeds with 0% loss and sub‑millisecond RTTs (PC15->PC16 avg ~0.72 ms; PC16->PC15 avg ~0.54 ms). Both hosts have correct IPs in the same /24 (172.18.51.15/24 and .16/24) and default gateway 172.18.51.254, which also responds normally to ping from PC15. ARP tables on both PCs show each other’s MAC addresses present, indicating proper L2 resolution. On the access switch (switch10), ports eth1 (PC15) and eth2 (PC16) are UP, part of bridge br0, VLAN 1 untagged, with error-free counters. This confirms the local switching path is operational.

Mechanism: If there had been an L2 issue between these adjacent hosts, pings would continue to fail and ARP would be incomplete; instead, bridging and ARP resolution are both healthy. Localization: PC15 (cd_sw10_fa1_1), PC16 (cd_sw10_fa2_1), and switch10 br0 show normal operation.

Root cause: The reported outage is not reproducible now; links and switching fabric are functioning, suggesting no current network fault (likely a transient condition that has cleared).

Corrective fix: No changes required. Connectivity is restored and stable. If the file sharing application still misbehaves, check host-level services/firewalls on PC15/PC16. If the network symptom recurs, capture exact time and we will monitor ARP/port-state on switch10 for transient flaps. 
=== END ===